WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:07.080
This paper has a ton of promise. Amazing&nbsp;
promises. Get this: it promises to reconstruct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:14.040
3D objects from a bunch of images. It&nbsp;
promises better generative 3D modeling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:22.160
even creating an animated 3D model of us moving,&nbsp;
tetrahedral mesh geometry for physics simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:30.040
for squishy things, and can also recommend&nbsp;
geometry models that are easier to 3D print.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:36.240
Now, wait wait wait. This is not&nbsp;
new. In fact, this is old stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:45.360
because this was possible for 36 years now. What?&nbsp;
Yes, really. The legendary computer graphics paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:52.760
by the name Marching Cubes also set out to do&nbsp;
that. It is a simple handcrafted technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.920
and it can be used for tomography,&nbsp;
magnetic resonance imaging and more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:05.600
It gives you everything you want. Or, Neural&nbsp;
Dual Contouring. A paper from just a year ago.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:12.960
So why not just use them? Well, here is why.&nbsp;
Look. We are starting out from this sphere and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:20.200
would like to morph it into this object.&nbsp;
Can we? Oof! Not quite! The previous Dual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:26.560
Contouring technique blows up immediately.&nbsp;
The neural version is more well behaved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:35.880
but it can’t give us our desired shape. Marching&nbsp;
cubes, comes to the rescue…until it falters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:42.160
being flexible enough to reconstruct the&nbsp;
final shape. A good try though. So, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:50.840
about the new technique? Wow, that is a seemingly&nbsp;
perfect reconstruction of the desired geometry.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:56.160
So, what else can it do? Well, hold&nbsp;
on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:02.680
because here in goes a video of a&nbsp;
physics simulation, this is really tough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:08.640
because but the desired geometry is&nbsp;
not stationary anymore. And once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:17.480
it starts out from a ball, and over time,&nbsp;
wow, look! Out comes 3D geometry that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:26.080
use in our video games and all kinds of virtual&nbsp;
worlds. All this from an input video. So cool!

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:33.440
And, it gets even better! When combined with a&nbsp;
text to 3D technique from just 2 years ago, here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:41.160
we can do something similar to text to image, we&nbsp;
write something, and out comes a painting of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:48.920
but here, instead we write something, and out&nbsp;
comes…can that really be? Out comes this 3D&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:55.000
geometry. And the difference is that finally,&nbsp;
we are now getting high-quality geometry.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:02.040
And get this, even creating animations is also&nbsp;
possible with this. That is a huge step forward.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:06.480
And because this is a differentiable&nbsp;
mesh generation technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.040
it means that it starts out from&nbsp;
something completely different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:18.840
and it slowly morphs into the final result over&nbsp;
time. I absolutely love these animations. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:24.200
this one shows that the new technique once&nbsp;
again, converges to the desired geometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:32.520
we have seen that, okay, but. Wait a minute.&nbsp;
That is not only a better piece of geometry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:40.280
but it also uses about 20% fewer little&nbsp;
elements, little triangles to accomplish that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:48.240
This means that these models will require less&nbsp;
memory, and can be rendered faster. Fantastic!

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.800
And there is an problem here. Do you see&nbsp;
the problem? Well, Marching Cubes has some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:02.680
more issues, for instance in the nose region here.&nbsp;
Thus, this geometry is typically not 3D printable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:10.200
or if so, not out of the box. And finally,&nbsp;
the new one can generate pieces of geometry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.360
that don’t have these problems, and&nbsp;
are now more suitable for 3D printing.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:21.880
Now, clearly, these are not yet the kinds&nbsp;
of assets that you would directly put into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:28.600
those triple A quality games, not even close.&nbsp;
But all this is a heck of a lot of value in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:35.560
just one paper. And just imagine what we will be&nbsp;
capable of just two more papers down the line.

